## Case 2: The Vulnerable HttpOnly-Bypass Target Structure
In this configuration, the site uses an HttpOnly token (managed behind the scenes by the server headers), meaning document.cookie returns completely blank. However, because the app features an unvalidated query router, an attacker's script can trigger background fetch routines. [3] 

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Secure HttpOnly Session - Unsafe Client Router</title>
</head>
<body>

    <div id="app">
        <h2>Welcome to the Account Portal</h2>
        <p>Your session cookies are protected with HttpOnly. JavaScript cannot read them.</p>
        
        <!-- Simulation box representing a sensitive profile setting form -->
        <div id="settings-panel">
            <p>Current Contact Email: <span id="current-email">user@target-domain.com</span></p>
        </div>
        
        <div id="alert-banner"></div>
    </div>

    <script>
        // Capture the incoming search/routing parameter
        const queryParams = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
        const routeData = queryParams.get('search');

        // VULNERABLE ROUTER SINK: Direct reflection allows an arbitrary script injection.
        // Even though cookies are hidden, this injected payload can execute a backend state change.
        if (routeData) {
            const outputFrame = document.getElementById('alert-banner');
            
            // If the query contains an injection payload, it runs with full system context
            outputFrame.innerHTML = `Search tracking reference active: ${routeData}`;
        }

        // --- SIMULATED BACKGROUND ENDPOINT THAT AN ATTACKER TARGETS VIA FETCH ---
        // This function represents the backend API handling update requests
        async function handleMockEmailUpdateAPI(newEmail) {
            console.log(`[API RECEIVER]: Request incoming to change account email to: ${newEmail}`);
            console.log(`[API RECEIVER]: Browser automatically attached the HttpOnly Session Cookie! Authorization Valid.`);
            document.getElementById('current-email').innerText = newEmail;
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>

